WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.760 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
In this video, I'll answer the questions I
get asked most often when I first speak to

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:04.400 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
a founder.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.320 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
So you get a clear picture
of what I actually do, who

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.750 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
it's for, and how it
works. And you can decide

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.360 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
for yourself whether it's right for
you before we even get on the call.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.200 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I work with founder-led businesses,
usually somewhere between 1 and 25 million

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.670 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
in revenue. They've grown well, but the
owner is still the one approving most

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.530 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
things. So if the important decisions
still come back to you, if your team waits

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.980 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
for your input before they act, and if
you couldn't step away for 2 or 3 weeks

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.000 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
without things starting
to slip, this is for you.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.760 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
What I usually see is a business that's
past the early stage, but the way it's run

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.240 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
hasn't caught up with how big it's got.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.090 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
That's where I come in. You lead the
change, and I work alongside you to build

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.370 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
the systems and processes that let your
team run the day-to-day, so you can get

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.340 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
back to strategy. There
are a few signs I look for.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.080 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
The first is decisions, if
the ones that should sit

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.070 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
with your team still land
on your desk, that's a

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.746 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
sign. The second is where
your processes live, if

00:01:03.746 --> 00:01:05.550 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
they live in people's
heads or scattered across

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.400 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
documents and nobody could
pick one up and follow it,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.290 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
that's another. The third
is that if you've already

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.968 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
tried to fix it, maybe you
hired someone or brought

00:01:13.968 --> 00:01:17.030 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
in some software and the
chaos underneath is still

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.060 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
the same. And the last one
is when the same problems

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.490 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
keep coming back, month
after month, because

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.600 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
nothing stops them
at the source.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.580 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
None of this is about ability. It's what
happens when a business outgrows the way

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.400 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
of working that got it off the ground.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.060 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Most growing businesses hit this stage.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.200 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
The founders who move past it are the ones
who build the structure to replace it, and

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.500 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
that's what we'll work on together.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.460 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I don't use the word "coaching".

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.900 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Coaching tends to be about
mindset and accountability.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.300 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Someone asking you good questions
but never getting their hands dirty.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.220 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Traditional consulting is the other end.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:55.530 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Someone hands you a framework or does the
work for you and the minute they leave,

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.540 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
nothing sticks, because your
team never learned to run it.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.510 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
What I do sits in between. You get
structure, the challenge and the

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:06.340 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
accountability, but you also get
real systems and hands-on help.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.160 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
And you and your team build it into the
business yourselves, with me alongside

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.140 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
you, so it's still there
long after we've finished.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.780 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
That's why I call it advisory. With you.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:21.220 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I'm not going to come in and take over
your operations, but I'm not going to hand

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.860 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
you a plan and disappear either.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.540 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I've seen founders given a polished
framework and then left alone to make it

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.140 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
work. That's not how I do
it. The systems are yours.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.470 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
You and your team build them in, and
I'm right there with you, because

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:38.140 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
implementation is the hard part,
and it's where most people give up.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.949 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Depending on where you are at, I might
spend time on site early on, and I stay

00:02:41.949 --> 00:02:45.460 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
with you through the engagement until
the systems are actually running.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:51.520 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Not just written down. Depends on where
you are starting from, your team and how

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.540 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
disciplined you are about doing the work.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.380 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
But I can tell you what
you are building towards.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.930 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
A business that doesn't
need you stepping in every

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.000 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
five minutes, you getting
real hours back in your

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.520 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
week, decisions going to
the right people instead

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.880 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
of landing on your desk,
your core processes

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.670 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
written down so someone
else can follow them, and

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.190 line:83% position:75% size:46% align:center
a proper handle on your
numbers and your cash.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.688 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Less than you think. I know time is the
worry, because it's probably why you are

00:03:18.688 --> 00:03:20.620 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
looking at this in the first place.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.560 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
In practice, it's a session with me every
couple of weeks, plus some focused work in

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.980 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
between, and your team carries
as much of that as you do.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.260 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I'm not adding another job to your plate.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.780 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
The whole point is to take work off you.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.750 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
The time you put in early is what
buys you those hours back later.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.100 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
That's what the first call is
for, and it works both ways.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.380 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
30 minutes, and it's not a sales pitch.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.860 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I'll ask where you are at, and I'll
tell you straight whether I can help.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.900 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
If I'm not the right person
for what you need, I'll say so.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.820 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
I'd much rather do that than take
on a client I can't get results for.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.420 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
So I hope that's answered
most of your questions.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:02.240 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
If there's anything I haven't covered,
don't worry, on the call I'm happy to go

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.140 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
into as much detail as you need.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.950 line:83% position:50% size:86% align:center
Thanks very much for watching,
and I'll speak to you soon.
